Changed for HW5:
In order to implement loading in images of different file formats, only one line of the controller was changed. Instead of calling a method to read a PPM file with a given filepath, a different method is called that parses several file types with the given filepath.
To implement saving images to different file formats, the "save" case in the ControllerImpl class was edited slightly. Instead of calling a method in the ImageUtil class that strictly creates PPM files, it calls a new method that saves an Image to a given file type, which can be either PPM, JPG, JPEG, PNG, or BMP. A few private helper methods were written in the ImageUtil class to accomplish this.

To implement the filtering and color transformation features, we included the following cases to the switch statement in our controller:
"blur", "sharpen", "grayscale", and "sepia." Each of these cases are structured the same way as previous cases. Our controller tells our Image (the model) to perform the filtering on the Image object in the controller.

To implement these features in our model, we added the methods blur, sharpen, grayscaleColorTransform, and sepia to the Image interface. We implemented these features in the ImageImpl class. We did this because the new features function similar to the previous features. The logic for grayscale and sepia is in the Pixel interface, so we added these new methods to the Pixel interface. Depending on which method was called in the ImageImpl class, the corresponding method is called in the PixelImpl class that performs the filter on every pixel in the image.

We also created a new interface named Filter that has one method, applyKernelToPixel, which applies a kernel of any size to a given Pixel in an Image. We created this interface to be able to apply filters like blurring and sharpening to an Image. This interface is implemented in the FilterImpl class. This class has one constructor that initializes an Image, a 2D Array representing the kernel to use for the filter, and a row and column value for the Pixel in the Image that the kernel is being applied to. Thus, to blur or sharpen, the ImageImpl class creates a new FilterImpl object and passes itself as the Image, a 2D array representing kernel for the filter we want (blur or sharpen or another filter that can be implemented in the future), and the row and column position for a Pixel in the image. The method applyKernelToPixel is then called on the FilterImpl object for each Pixel in the Image.

If a new filter is to be implemented in the future, a new method would be added to the Image interface, and the implementation of the method would be the same as previous filters.

In the PixelImpl class, we also edited the constructor so that each component is limited to be between 0 and max value, inclusive. This way, if a filter attempts to create a pixel with an invalid component, it will automatically adjust the value to be between 0 and the pixel's max value.

In our Main method in the ImageProcessor class where the program starts, we added functionality to allow the user to provide a command-line argument to specify a script using the format "-file name-of-script.txt". If a valid file is provided, the program will run the script and exit. Otherwise the program will use the interactive entry of script commands as before.


From HW4:
The structure of our program follows the MVC design. The program starts in the ImageProcessor class, where a controller object is created and its run method is called.

Controller:
Our Controller interface consists of one public method called run that takes no arguments. The purpose of this interface is to control the flow of the program by handling user input. It delegates tasks to the view and model accordingly. This interface is implemented in the ControllerImpl class, which implements the run method. When called, this method runs the program. An IOException is thrown if any output is unable to reach the Appendable.

The Controller interface is implemented by the ControllerImpl class. Objects of this class have a View object, a Readable object as its input source, and a HashMap from String to Image representing the list of images and the name that is used to refer to them as a String. This class implements the run method which throws an IOException if output cannot be transmitted to the view. This class also have two private helper methods. One of them returns an image from the HashMap according to the String name it was passed, and the other one turns the given Image into a StringBuilder that represents the image in PPM format.

Model:
Our model is represented by the Image interface, which represents an image that can be manipulated using our program. This interface provides methods to visualize an image in grayscale using its red component, green component, blue component, value, intensity, and luma values. It also provides functionality to flip an image vertically or horizontally and to brighten or darken an image by a specific value. This interface also has methods to get the width, height, max value, and a specific pixel of the image based on the given row and column. The ImageImpl class implements the Image interface and its methods. It has a 2D ArrayList of the Pixel interface to represent its image.

The Pixel interface represents a pixel in an Image. This interface's methods that deal with changing a pixel return a new Pixel instead of changing the original pixel. This is because when manipulating images, users are never editing the contents of the image they are "editing," they are simply creating a new image with the edits applied. The original image always remains unchanged. The other methods in this interface are simply getter methods that return an int representing the component of the Pixel that is being obtained. Because they are simply ints, these getter methods also do not allow for any mutation. The only method that does not fall into the two categories is the equals and hashcode method.

The PixelImpl class implements the Pixel interface. Objects of this class represent pixels that has 3 channels: red, green, and blue, along with a maxValue, which represents the maximum value of any channel. The 3 channels are always between 0 and maxValue, inclusive. This class implements the methods in the Pixel interface while upholding the immutability of this class's fields.

View:
Our view is represented by the View interface. This interface provides operations that deal with sending information to the user. It has two methods: renderMessage that takes in a String message and showOptions that takes in no arguments. renderMessage outputs the given message to the user and throws an IOException if the output was unable to be sent. showOptions shows a menu of possible commands to the user. This method also throws an IOException if the output could not be sent.

The View interface is implemented in the TextView class, which represents a way of showing messages to the user using an Appendable. If not provided an Appendable, this class defaults to using System.out. This class implements renderMessage and showOptions in the View interface.


The image "fourPixels.ppm" was created by Angela Zheng using Gimp. I authorize the use of this image for this project.


How to use our program:

0) No need to give command line arguments
1) Load in your desired image by doing the command: load image-path image-name
2) Then you may edit/transform your image with any of the following commands:
    - Red-Component: red-component image-name dest-image-name    
    - Green-Component: green-component image-name dest-image-name    
    - Blue-Component: blue-component image-name dest-image-name    
    - Value: value image-name dest-image-name    
    - Luma: luma image-name dest-image-name    
    - Intensity: intensity image-name dest-image-name    
    - Horizontal Flip: horizontal-flip image-name dest-image-name    
    - Vertical Flip: vertical-flip image-name dest-image-name    
    - Brighten: brighten increment image-name dest-image-name    
    - Darken: darken increment image-name dest-image-name
3) Save your image by doing the command: save image-path image-name
4) Finally you can quit by typing q or Q
